    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0,
        "text": "19 October 1812. After five weeks in burned Moscow, Napoleon leaves with about 100,000 men and thousands of carts of loot. He means to go home by a southern road, through country not yet stripped."
      },
      {
        "t": 5,
        "text": "At Maloyaroslavets Kutuzov's army blocks the way south. Napoleon turns back north to the road he came by, through land both armies have already laid waste."
      },
      {
        "t": 10,
        "text": "The column stretches for miles. Horses die for want of fodder, guns are abandoned, and Cossacks pick off anyone who strays to forage."
      },
      {
        "t": 16,
        "text": "Early November brings hard frosts and snow. Discipline breaks down; men fight each other for food and shelter."
      },
      {
        "t": 21,
        "text": "Smolensk was meant to be a haven with food stores. Most are plundered in the first hours. The army leaves on 14 November."
      },
      {
        "t": 28,
        "text": "At Krasnoi the Russians try to cut the road. The Guard fights its way through; Ney's rearguard is cut off and escapes across the frozen Dnieper with a few hundred men."
      },
      {
        "t": 34,
        "text": "Chichagov's army from the south has taken Borisov and its bridge over the Berezina. Wittgenstein closes from the north. The trap is almost shut."
      },
      {
        "t": 37,
        "text": "At Studianka engineers build two bridges, standing in the freezing river. For three days the army crosses under fire. On 29 November the bridges are burned, leaving thousands of stragglers behind."
      },
      {
        "t": 44,
        "text": "Temperatures fall below −30 °C. On 5 December at Smorgon Napoleon hands command to Murat and leaves by sleigh for Paris, to raise a new army."
      },
      {
        "t": 50,
        "text": "Vilna's stores could have fed the army for weeks, but the starving crowd overwhelms them. Thousands die in the streets."
      },
      {
        "t": 54,
        "text": "On 14 December Ney crosses the Niemen with the last of the rearguard. Of more than 600,000 men who invaded Russia, perhaps 100,000 come back."
      }
    ]
  }
